{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg1WcNo3oBNjPBwN5jAg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I get **Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 611 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 4.9 × 10³ cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 8.0)**. Dq and B follow exactly from the two spin-allowed quartet bands. C depends on an approximate expression for the ²E band, so it is only reliable to about ±100–200 cm⁻¹.\n\n**1. Dq from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g band**\n\nFor octahedral d³, the first spin-allowed band equals Δₒ:\n- 10Dq = 17 500 cm⁻¹\n- Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹\n\n**2. B from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(F) band**\n\nThe second quartet band is:\n\nν₂ = 15Dq + 7.5B − ½√(225B² − 180·Dq·B + 100Dq²)\n\n1. Insert ν₂ = 23 800 cm⁻¹ and 15Dq = 26 250 cm⁻¹. This gives 7.5B + 2450 = ½√(225B² − 315 000B + 3.0625 × 10⁸).\n2. Square both sides. The B² terms cancel, leaving 462 000B = 2.8224 × 10⁸.\n3. Therefore B ≈ 611 cm⁻¹. This is about 67% of the free-ion value of 918 cm⁻¹, a typical nephelauxetic reduction for oxalate.\n\n**3. C from the spin-forbidden ⁴A₂g → ²E_g band**\n\nI used Griffith's strong-field expression, which includes configuration mixing only to second order:\n\nE(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/(10Dq)\n\n1. 14 300 = 5498 + 3C − 1919\n2. 3C ≈ 14 721\n3. C ≈ 4.9 × 10³ cm⁻¹\n\nThe precise value would be about 4907 cm⁻¹. However, a full diagonalisation of the ²E matrices would shift C by roughly 100–200 cm⁻¹, so I've given it to two significant figures.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1750, \"B\": 611, \"C\": 4900, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq and B come exactly from the quartet bands; C comes from the approximate strong-field 2E expression.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":820,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics"